WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.537
Let's talk about risk factors
and protective factors.

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.807
When someone goes through
a situation of intense stress

3
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:12.497
or potential trauma, how they react
does not depend only on the event itself.

4
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.877
It also depends on a set
of factors that exist before,

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.357
during and after the situation.

6
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.477
And these can increase the risk
of lasting distress or, on the contrary,

7
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.501
support a healthy recovery.

8
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.917
Before the crisis:
prior vulnerability and protection.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.757
Even before the event happens,

10
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.017
some people already
have vulnerability factors.

11
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.027
These include a history of trauma

12
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.717
or adverse experiences throughout life,

13
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.490
as well as previously
fragile mental health.

14
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.327
These elements can increase
sensitivity to stress

15
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.277
and make emotional regulation
harder in demanding situations.

16
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.387
On the other hand,

17
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:57.317
there are also prior protective factors
that can soften the impact of a crisis.

18
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.727
These include positive social bonds,

19
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:06.027
with family, colleagues or the community
at large, as well as emotional literacy

20
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.952
and the use of effective
coping strategies.

21
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.997
Having overcome hardship before

22
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.277
and organisational settings
marked by a culture of care,

23
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.517
prevention and listening
are also protective elements.

24
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.297
These factors do not eliminate
the impact of the crisis,

25
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.277
but they significantly
influence how it is experienced.

26
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.917
During the crisis:
the immediate response of the context.

27
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.377
During a critical moment,
such as an accident,

28
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.367
an intense conflict, a dismissal
or another destabilising event,

29
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.658
factors in the immediate context
can worsen or ease the distress.

30
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.187
A lack of clear
and empathetic communication,

31
00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.677
disorganised or absent leadership,

32
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.767
the emotional isolation
of the person affected, or punitive

33
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.654
and dismissive responses
from the organisation

34
00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.625
are important risk factors.

35
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.957
In contrast, immediate support can act

36
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.099
as a significant protective factor.

37
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.097
Being with someone
who listens with empathy,

38
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.407
acknowledgement of the impact
of the situation,

39
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.967
a physically and emotionally
safe space to react

40
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.817
and rapid intervention by someone trained

41
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.417
in psychological first aid are elements

42
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.417
that contribute to initial stabilisation.

43
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:23.947
In the workplace,

44
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.977
the way the organisation responds
at this moment can be decisive.

45
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.357
After the crisis: recovery and follow-up.

46
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.767
The weeks and months after
the situation are equally important

47
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.817
for the recovery process.

48
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.437
A quick return to activities
without adequate recovery time,

49
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.127
institutional silence or denial
of the impact of the situation,

50
00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.097
a lack of psychological support

51
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.157
and ongoing adverse working conditions,
such as excessive pressure,

52
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.257
conflict or isolation,
can worsen the distress.

53
00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.567
On the other hand,
follow-up by managers or peers,

54
00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.287
access to psychological support,
acts of care and solidarity and,

55
00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.720
where necessary,
adapting working conditions

56
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.277
act as important protective
factors at this stage.

57
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.777
In short, the impact of a crisis
does not depend only on the event itself,

58
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.207
but on all the individual and
organisational conditions that precede it,

59
00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:23.597
accompany it and follow it.

60
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.877
Acting on these factors allows

61
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.787
a more structured
approach to mental health,

62
00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.657
focused not only on responding to crises,

63
00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.473
but also on preventing
and managing them over time.
